625, -0.024200439453125, -0.0291595458984375, -0.0089111328125, -0.00897216796875, 0.00803375244140625, 0.02825927734375, -0.0021953582763671875, 0.0076904296875, 0.01715087890625, -0.0184783935546875, -0.020782470703125, 0.0380859375, 0.0031147003173828125, 0.04669189453125, 0.00274658203125, 0.015838623046875, -0.0227203369140625, 0.039642333984375, 0.005992889404296875, -0.002132415771484375, 0.005138397216796875, -0.01439666748046875, -0.00836181640625, 0.01474761962890625, 0.003635406494140625, -0.0177459716796875, 0.033660888671875, 0.0016660690307617188, 0.0245208740234375, -0.0076446533203125, -0.0010967254638671875, -0.01169586181640625, 0.0006165504455566406, 0.0206756591796875, -0.01531219482421875, 0.016326904296875, -0.004077911376953125, -0.019989013671875, 0.014862060546875, -0.03472900390625, -0.01523590087890625, 0.012451171875, -0.0066375732421875, 0.006549835205078125, 0.0025920867919921875, 0.03411865234375, 0.041015625, -0.0086517333984375, -0.01195526123046875, -0.0029659271240234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024 from a single academic institution. No external datasets were used for model development or validation.\n\nThe multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three components. (1) A rule-based agent used regular expressions (regex) to identify and label standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level. For example, if a sentence contained any term from a curated \u201clungs\u201d list, it was labeled as \u201clungs.\u201d Anatomical term lists were mapped to sections with explicit precedence rules when terms overlapped (e.g., a sentence mentioning both \u201cliver\u201d and \u201ckidney\u201d would follow a predetermined hierarchy). (2) A large language model (Llama 2 13B) was locally deployed and further fine-tuned on 15,000 de-identified in-house radiology reports (not overlapping with the evaluation set). Llama 2 13B was chosen for its strong accuracy and computational efficiency for local deployment compared to smaller or significantly larger models. Fine-tuning hyperparameters (learning rate 2e-5, batch size 8, three epochs, 10% validation split) were selected based on preliminary experiments showing stable convergence and optimal per-label F1 score on held-out data while avoiding overfitting. Model selection prioritized the lowest validation loss and highest per-label F1. (3) QA agents detected discrepancies using deterministic rules, applied as follows: for section mismatches, the system flagged any abnormality sentence present in the Findings but absent from the Impression (and vice versa), based on keyword co-occurrence and negation detection (e.g., \u201clung nodule\u201d in Findings with no mention in Impression). For gender-anatomy conflicts, the agent cross-referenced patient demographic metadata with report text (e.g., a sentence referencing \u201cprostate\u201d in a report for a recorded female patient triggered a flag). For undocumented communication, reports were flagged if phrases such as \u201ccritical finding,\u201d \u201curgent,\u201d or \u201cimmediate communication\u201d appeared in the Findings or Impression sections, but no documentation of provider notification was noted (e.g., absence of \u201cprovider notified\u201d or equivalent phrase). All thresholds were binary: presence/absence of terms, without probabilistic or ML-based criteria. Inline examples illustrate each rule type above for clarity.\n\nEvaluation: A stratified random subset of 45 reports was selected for manual review, ensuring direct representation across three QA-flag categories: section mismatch (n=20), gender-anatomy conflict (n=10), and undocumented communication (n=10), plus 5 unflagged reports, to balance sample exposure. Two board-certified radiologists independently evaluated each structured output according to four metrics with explicit operational definitions: \u201cfidelity\u201d (whether all information and intended meaning of the original report was preserved verbatim or paraphrased without loss), \u201caccuracy\u201d (correct assignment of each sentence to its appropriate anatomical section and correct abnormality classification), \u201comission\u201d (absence of any clinically important finding or fact present in the original report), and \u201cfabrication\u201d (presence of any statement not found in or inferred from the original text). Metrics were assessed as follows: for fidelity and accuracy, reviewers compared structured outputs sentence-by-sentence to the original; for omission and fabrication, any discrepancies were flagged and reviewed for clinical significance.\n\nReviewers were blinded to QA flag status. Disagreements were resolved via discussion; if consensus was not reached after discussion, a third board-certified radiologist adjudicated. Of the 45 reports, consensus was reached by the two reviewers in 40 cases (89%); the third reviewer adjudicated the remaining 5 cases (11%). Consensus was defined as identical rating assignment for all four metrics.\n\nSample size was determined by reviewer workload feasibility and the need to include all flag categories, not by formal power analysis; the resulting pilot sample provides an approximate 95% confidence interval for key proportions but yields a margin of error of \u00b113\u201315%. The small sample increases risk of sampling bias and may overestimate performance due to possible overfitting to institution-specific language.\n\nRatings for overall QA performance were defined as follows: \u201cexcellent\u201d (no errors, high clarity, structure, and correctness according to all metrics), \u201cgood\u201d (minor errors not affecting clinical interpretation or utility), and \u201cfair\u201d (noticeable deficiencies in structuring, though not resulting in clinically significant omissions or fabrications).\n\nResults: The multi-agent system restructured the Findings sections of all reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining original content. The system flagged 90 (14.1%) reports: 80 (12.5%) for section mismatches, 5 (0.8%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 5 (0.8%) for undocumented communication of critical findings using the rule-driven criteria. Examples include a \u201crenal mass\u201d in Findings with no mention in Impression (\u201csection mismatch\u201d), reference to \u201cprostate\u201d for a female patient (\u201cgender-anatomy conflict\u201d), and \u201curgent pulmonary embolism\u201d described without communication statement (\u201cundocumented communication\u201d).\n\nIn radiologist evaluation of the 45-report subset, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) were incorrectly restructured, and there was disagreement on 12 reports (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%). The Cohen\u2019s kappa for inter-reviewer agreement was 0.49 (moderate agreement). Consensus was reached for 40 reports after initial review; 5 cases required third reviewer adjudication. No clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report (by consensus across metrics). Final QA performance rating was \u201cexcellent\u201d in 38% (n=17) of reports, \u201cgood\u201d in 46% (n=21), and \u201cfair\u201d in 16% (n=7), using the criteria above. No formal hypothesis testing was performed due to limited power of the pilot sample.\n\nDiscussion: This study is limited by a single-institution dataset, lack of external validation, and a small, stratified manual review sample. These factors restrict generalizability to diverse report styles, institutions, or patient populations, and increase the likelihood of selection and reviewer bias or overfitting to local report conventions. Future work should reduce bias by blinding reviewers to additional metadata, enrolling multiple sites with varied reporting practices, using larger samples, and randomizing case selection more broadly. External validation and benchmarking against alternative structuring approaches are necessary to assess robustness, reliability, and clinical utility.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system integrated radiology report structuring and quality assurance using deterministic rule sets and a fine-tuned local language model. In a pilot radiologist evaluation of 45 stratified reports, the system showed favorable structuring performance with moderate reviewer agreement, no clinically important errors, and a majority of ratings in the \u201cexcellent\u201d or \u201cgood\u201d categories. Limitations include a small, pilot review sample and use of a single-institution dataset; broader validation is essential to determine generalizability and clinical impact. Such systems may support standardization and quality assurance in radiology, but further, more extensive studies are needed."}}